"""Service detail grounded in Middletown's published service and conversion pages."""
def expand(services,providers):
    services['oil-to-gas-conversion']=dict(label='Oil-to-gas & heating conversions',key='hvac',eyebrow='A DIFFERENT WAY TO HEAT HOME.',title='Ready to move<br>beyond heating oil?',desc='Compare oil-to-gas conversions, heat pumps, propane and dual-fuel heating with Middletown HVAC in Delco and the Main Line.',intro='Another oil delivery can get you thinking. Middletown HVAC helps compare a full conversion with smaller changes that fit the house you already have.',sections=[
    ('Oil to gas starts with the house—and the gas supply.','<p>Natural gas is one option when service is available. Before choosing equipment, discuss utility availability, the existing furnace or boiler, distribution system, venting and hot-water setup. A conversion proposal should explain which parts of the existing system stay and what changes.</p><p>Middletown’s published conversion options include natural gas, heat pumps, propane and dual fuel. The right conversation starts with your home rather than one preferred fuel.</p>'),
    ('Compare a full conversion with a smaller step.','<ul><li><strong>Oil to natural gas:</strong> discuss fuel availability and a complete installation scope.</li><li><strong>Heat pump:</strong> compare heating and cooling together, with the house and existing distribution in mind.</li><li><strong>Hybrid or dual fuel:</strong> discuss when backup heating would operate and which equipment remains.</li><li><strong>Ductless:</strong> consider rooms that need their own heating and cooling without new ductwork.</li><li><strong>Separate hot water:</strong> ask whether changing a summer-winter hookup could reduce the need to run the oil boiler just for hot water.</li></ul>'),
    ('What belongs in the estimate?','<p>Ask about heating-load calculations, ductwork or hydronic distribution, electrical capacity, controls, utility work, and the written scope for the oil tank. Confirm who handles removal or decommissioning, permits and any additional trade work. Compare the complete project rather than equipment prices alone.</p><p>Operating costs depend on your house, usage and local energy rates. Bring recent fuel-use information if you have it; a particular saving or payback period should not be assumed.</p><p><a href="https://www.mtownhvac.com/conversion/">See Middletown’s conversion options and planning tools ↗</a> · <a href="/services/ductless-mini-splits/">Explore ductless mini splits</a></p>')],faqs=[('Can Middletown convert oil heating to gas?','Middletown lists natural-gas conversions among its options. Contact the team to confirm utility availability and the installation scope for your home.'),('Do I have to remove every part of my oil system?','Not necessarily. Middletown discusses full conversions and hybrid options. Ask what stays, what changes and how each option would operate.'),('Can a boiler home use a heat pump?','Ask for an assessment of the house and its distribution system. Ductless, separate hot water and full-system changes can involve different scopes.')])
    services['ductless-mini-splits']=dict(label='Ductless mini-split installation & service',key='hvac',eyebrow='COMFORT, ROOM BY ROOM.',title='That one room.<br>Comfortable at last.',desc='Explore ductless mini-split installation, repairs and maintenance with Middletown HVAC for Delco, Chester County and Main Line homes.',intro='The bedroom that never cools down. The addition with no ducts. The office you use all day. A ductless conversation starts with the space you want to enjoy.',sections=[
    ('A useful option when ducts are not the answer.','<p>Middletown’s ductless services cover installation, service and maintenance. Its website describes options for converted attics, additions, bonus rooms, sunrooms, bedrooms, offices, garages and homes without ductwork.</p><p>Tell the team how you use the room, what heating or cooling it has today, and whether you want one space improved or a broader plan.</p>'),
    ('One room or several zones?','<p>A single-zone system and a multi-zone project are different decisions. Discuss indoor-unit locations, outdoor-unit placement, refrigerant-line routing, drainage, electrical work and access for maintenance. Ask how equipment selection relates to the needs of each room.</p><p>For an oil-heated home, ductless may also be part of a conversation about reducing oil use. Compare that plan with <a href="/services/oil-to-gas-conversion/">other heating-conversion options</a>.</p>'),
    ('Plan for the years after installation.','<p>Ask what maintenance applies to the proposed system, how indoor units are cleaned and how service is scheduled. If an existing mini split is noisy, leaking or no longer keeping up, describe the change and request a service assessment.</p><p><a href="https://www.mtownhvac.com/services/ductless/">Read Middletown’s ductless service details ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.mtownhvac.com/ductless-estimator/">Explore Middletown’s ductless estimator ↗</a></p>')],faqs=[('Can a mini split heat and cool a room?','Ductless heat-pump systems can provide heating and cooling. Confirm the proposed equipment and its application to your space with Middletown.'),('Do you need existing ductwork?','Ductless systems serve rooms without using conventional ductwork. The installation still needs suitable equipment locations, electrical supply, drainage and line routing.'),('Can I request service for an existing system?','Middletown publishes ductless service and maintenance options. Share the brand, model and symptoms so the office can confirm the appropriate visit.')])
    providers['middletown']['body']+='<h2>Thinking beyond a repair?</h2><p><a href="/services/oil-to-gas-conversion/">Oil-to-gas and heating conversions</a> can include gas, propane, heat-pump or dual-fuel options. For additions, hard-to-condition rooms and homes without ducts, explore <a href="/services/ductless-mini-splits/">ductless mini-split installation and service</a>.</p><h2>The full home-comfort picture.</h2><p>Middletown’s published services also include air conditioning, furnaces, heat pumps, hot-water and steam boilers, water heating, indoor air quality, comfort controls and water treatment. Discuss the problem, existing equipment and the result you want; the team confirms the scope for your home.</p><p><a href="https://www.mtownhvac.com/services/">Explore the full Middletown HVAC service menu ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.mtownhvac.com/install-estimate/">See published installation pricing ↗</a></p>'
    providers['liberator']['body']+='<h2>The everyday things that make home work.</h2><p>A dripping faucet, a fixture ready for an update, a drain that keeps slowing down—plumbing problems tend to make themselves part of your day. Liberator’s listed services span fixtures, pipe repairs, drains and sewer inquiries. Start with what you’re noticing and what you want to improve.</p><p>For a planned project, describe the fixtures, access and timing. For a recurring problem, explain what has already been tried. That gives the business a useful starting point for discussing the visit.</p>'
    providers['cooke']['body']+='<h2>For the parts of home you notice every day.</h2><p>The unfinished detail. The small repair you keep walking past. Shelving or trim that would make a room work better. Cooke’s Wazoozie profile is a starting point for smaller carpentry and home-repair conversations, with direct contact so you can explain what you have in mind.</p><p>Share photos, approximate dimensions and the result you want. Ask which parts of the project fit the business’s scope, how the work would be scheduled and what the written proposal covers.</p>'
    providers['ryan']['body']+='<h2>Make the room feel right.</h2><p>Lighting is part of how a home feels, whether it’s a welcoming entry, a work area you can finally see clearly or an outdoor space you want to enjoy after dark. Bring Ryan your ideas as well as the practical problem. Ask about fixture placement, controls and the electrical work behind the finished look.</p>'
